;; Docs for std/units.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "units.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Units of measure: SI declared for typed quantities, and measurements at run time.")
  (see convert-query string->measurement resolve-unit standard-units)
  (example """
    (import (std units))

    ;; Typed: the units are checked at compile time and gone at run time.
    (: drag (-> (double kg/m^3) (double m/s) double (double m^2) (double N)))
    (defun (drag rho v cd area) (* 0.5 rho v v cd area))

    (number-in (drag <1.225 kg/m^3> <120 km/h> 0.28 <2.2 m^2>) N)   ; 419.2222
    (number-in (+ <6000 m> <50 ft>) mi)                             ; 3.737697

    ;; At run time: a unit read from text, which no type knows.
    (convert-query "6000 m + 50 ft in mi")         ; (Ok "3.737697 mi")
    (convert-query "65 mi/(12 mi/h) in h,min")     ; (Ok "5 h 25 min")
    (convert-query "(2 kg/yr)/(3 m3/s) in ng/l")   ; (Ok "21.12539 ng/l")

    (match (string->measurement "120 km/h")
      ((Ok v) (measurement-in v "mph"))            ; (Ok 74.56454)
      ((Err e) (Err e)))
    """)
  (reference """
    The module is two things. It declares SI with @code{def/dimension},
    @code{def/prefix} and @code{def/unit}, which is what a quantity literal
    such as @code{<120 km/h>}, a type such as @code{(double m/s)} and
    @code{number-in} read units from: the manual's section on units of
    measure says how those work. And it is a library for units at run time,
    for a unit the program reads as text: a calculator, a configuration file.

    @strong{The declarations.} The dimensions and their base units:
    @code{length} (@code{m}), @code{mass} (@code{kg}), @code{time}
    (@code{s}), @code{current} (@code{A}), @code{temperature} (@code{K}),
    @code{amount} (@code{mol}), @code{luminosity} (@code{cd}) and
    @code{information} (@code{b}, bits, kept in a @code{long}). The SI
    prefixes from @code{q} to @code{Q}, and the binary ones from @code{Ki} to
    @code{Yi}. About 270 units and their spellings: time (@code{min},
    @code{h}, @code{d}, @code{yr}), length (@code{in}, @code{ft}, @code{mi},
    @code{nmi}, @code{au}, @code{ly}, @code{pc}), area, volume (@code{l},
    @code{gal}, @code{cup}), mass (@code{g}, @code{t}, @code{lb},
    @code{oz}), force, pressure (@code{N}, @code{Pa}, @code{bar},
    @code{atm}, @code{psi}), energy and power (@code{J}, @code{W}, @code{Wh},
    @code{cal}, @code{eV}), electricity, information (@code{B}),
    radiation (@code{Bq}, @code{Gy}, @code{Sv}), angles (@code{deg},
    @code{rev}, @code{rpm}, which are plain numbers: @code{rad} is 1), ratios
    (@code{%}, @code{ppm}) and the constants @code{c}, @code{gn} and
    @code{planck}, which are units: @code{(number-in v c)} is a speed as a
    fraction of light's. A program that wants other units declares them; a
    program that wants other bases declares dimensions of its own, and does
    not import this module.

    @strong{At run time.} A dimension is a product of base dimensions with
    rational exponents. A unit is an atom with a dimension and an exact
    factor to the coherent unit of that dimension, or an alias for a unit
    expression. A @code{Measurement} is a double in the coherent unit and its
    dimension.

    @strong{Grammar.} Tightest first: @code{a|b} divides two numbers; @code{^}
    (or @code{**}) raises to a rational exponent, as in @code{m^2},
    @code{Hz^(-1/2)} and @code{s^-1}; juxtaposition multiplies; @code{*} and
    @code{/} go left to right; @code{+} and @code{-} add measurements. So
    @code{kg m/s^2} is kg·m/s² and @code{m/s kg} is m/(s·kg). A name ending in
    digits is a power, @code{m2}, unless the whole name is a unit.
    @code{pi} is the number. @code{sqrt(...)} and @code{cbrt(...)} take roots
    of measurements.

    @strong{Names.} A name is looked up as written, then as a name with a
    trailing power, then as a prefix on a unit, the longest prefix first:
    @code{min} is a minute, @code{MiB} is 2^20 bytes. Plurals are aliases in
    the table, not guessed.

    @strong{The standard table.} The SI base dimensions and information
    (@code{b}), the SI and binary prefixes, and about 200 units. There is no
    @code{degC} or @code{degF}: absolute temperatures are @code{Celsius} and
    @code{Fahrenheit}, and differences are @code{K} or @code{degR}.

    @strong{Errors.} Functions that read text return
    @code{(Result string ...)}. Arithmetic on measurements of different
    dimensions, and the temperature and decibel functions on the wrong
    dimension, raise @code{ArgumentException}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Factors
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Factor
  (summary "An exact conversion factor: a rational times an integer power of pi."))

(:doc factor
  (summary "A factor.")
  (arg r "The rational part.")
  (arg k "The power of pi.")
  (returns "r × pi^k."))

(:doc factor-rational
  (summary "The rational part of a factor.")
  (arg f "The factor.")
  (returns "r in r × pi^k."))

(:doc factor-pi
  (summary "The power of pi in a factor.")
  (arg f "The factor.")
  (returns "k in r × pi^k."))

(:doc factor-one
  (summary "The factor 1."))

(:doc factor-one?
  (summary "Whether a factor is 1.")
  (arg f "The factor.")
  (returns "#t for 1 × pi^0."))

(:doc factor*
  (summary "The product of two factors.")
  (arg a "The first factor.")
  (arg b "The second factor.")
  (returns "a × b."))

(:doc factor/
  (summary "The quotient of two factors.")
  (arg a "The dividend.")
  (arg b "The divisor. Raises DivideByZeroException when its rational part is zero.")
  (returns "a / b."))

(:doc factor-expt
  (summary "A factor to a rational power, when the result is exact.")
  (arg f "The factor.")
  (arg q "The exponent.")
  (returns "(Some f^q), or None when f^q is irrational, or f is zero and q negative.")
  (example """
    (factor-expt (factor (rational 100 1) 0) (rational 1 2))   ; (Some 10)
    (factor-expt (factor (rational 1000 1) 0) (rational 1 2))  ; None
    """))

(:doc factor->double
  (summary "A factor as a double.")
  (arg f "The factor.")
  (returns "The nearest double to the rational part, times pi^k."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Atoms and resolved expressions
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Atom
  (summary "A named unit with its dimension and its exact factor to the coherent unit of that dimension."))

(:doc atom
  (summary "An atom.")
  (arg name "The unit's name.")
  (arg dims "Its dimension: base dimension names to exponents.")
  (arg f "Its factor to the coherent unit of dims.")
  (returns "The atom."))

(:doc atom-name
  (summary "The name of an atom.")
  (arg a "The atom.")
  (returns "The name."))

(:doc atom-dims
  (summary "The dimension of an atom.")
  (arg a "The atom.")
  (returns "Base dimension names to nonzero exponents."))

(:doc atom-factor
  (summary "The factor of an atom.")
  (arg a "The atom.")
  (returns "Its factor to the coherent unit of its dimension."))

(:doc Resolved
  (summary "A resolved unit expression: a coefficient times atoms to rational powers.")
  (reference """
    @code{resolve-unit} returns one. The term maps atom names to their
    exponents, and the atoms map holds every atom the term names, so a
    @code{Resolved} needs no table to be converted. @code{3 km/h} is the
    coefficient 3 and the term km·h⁻¹.
    """))

(:doc resolved-coeff
  (summary "The coefficient of a resolved expression.")
  (arg r "The resolved expression.")
  (returns "The number the expression multiplies its atoms by."))

(:doc resolved-term
  (summary "The atoms of a resolved expression and their exponents.")
  (arg r "The resolved expression.")
  (returns "Atom names to nonzero rational exponents."))

(:doc resolved-atoms
  (summary "The atoms a resolved expression names.")
  (arg r "The resolved expression.")
  (returns "Atom names to atoms, one for each name in the term."))

(:doc resolved-dims
  (summary "The dimension of a resolved expression.")
  (arg r "The resolved expression.")
  (returns "Base dimension names to nonzero exponents."))

(:doc resolved-factor
  (summary "The exact factor from a resolved expression to the coherent unit of its dimension.")
  (arg r "The resolved expression.")
  (returns "(Ok factor), or (Err message) when the factor is irrational.")
  (reference """
    The factor of @code{km/h} is 5/18. The factor of @code{km^(1/2)} is
    √1000, and the result is @code{(Err "the factor of km^(1/2) is
    irrational")}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Tables
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc UnitTable
  (summary "Dimensions, units, prefixes and preferred units for printing."))

(:doc PrefixUse
  (summary "Which prefixes printing may put on a preferred unit: NoPrefix, SmallPrefixes (below 1 only) or AllPrefixes."))

(:doc empty-unit-table
  (summary "A table with nothing in it."))

(:doc def-dimension
  (summary "Adds a base dimension and its coherent unit.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg dim "The dimension's name, as in \"length\".")
  (arg unit "The unit's name, as in \"m\".")
  (returns "The new table, or why the names cannot be added.")
  (example """
    (def-dimension empty-unit-table "money" "EUR")
    """))

(:doc def-unit
  (summary "Adds a unit defined by a unit expression.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg name "The unit's name.")
  (arg definition "A unit expression over the table, as in \"12 in\".")
  (returns "The new table, or why the unit cannot be added.")
  (reference """
    A definition with coefficient 1 makes an alias: @code{N} for
    @code{kg m/s^2} is the same unit as its definition. Any other makes an
    atom of its own: @code{ft} for @code{12 in}. The definition's factor must
    be exact. A name that is already defined is an error.
    """))

(:doc def-prefix
  (summary "Adds a prefix.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg name "The prefix, as in \"k\".")
  (arg value "Its value as a number, as in \"1000\", \"1e-3\" or \"1|1024\".")
  (returns "The new table, or why the prefix cannot be added."))

(:doc def-preferred
  (summary "Sets the unit printing uses for that unit's dimension.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg unit "A unit expression, usually one name.")
  (arg use "Which prefixes printing may put on it.")
  (returns "The new table, or why the unit does not resolve."))

(:doc unit-table-names
  (summary "The unit names of a table.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (returns "Every name defined in the table, prefixed and powered forms not included."))

(:doc standard-units
  (summary "The standard table: SI, binary prefixes, and the common units."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Resolving
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc resolve-unit
  (summary "Resolves a unit expression exactly against a table.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg text "The expression, as in \"km/h\" or \"1|60 deg\".")
  (returns "The resolved expression, or what is wrong with the text.")
  (reference """
    @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{sqrt} and @code{cbrt} are not unit
    expressions. A power whose coefficient would be irrational, as in
    @code{2^(1/2)}, is an error; an atom to a rational power is not.
    Unknown names come with the nearest name in the table when one is close:
    @code{unknown unit 'meeter'. Did you mean 'meter'?}.
    """))

(:doc conformable-units
  (summary "The names in a table with the same dimension as an expression: GNU's ?.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (arg text "A unit expression.")
  (returns "The names, or what is wrong with the text."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Measurements
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Measurement
  (summary "A value in the coherent unit of its dimension, and the dimension.")
  (field value "The value in the coherent unit.")
  (field dims "Base dimension names to nonzero exponents."))

(:doc measurement
  (summary "A measurement.")
  (arg v "The value in the coherent unit.")
  (arg d "The dimension: base dimension names to nonzero exponents.")
  (returns "The measurement."))

(:doc measurement-value
  (summary "The value of a measurement.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (returns "The value in the coherent unit of its dimension."))

(:doc measurement-dims
  (summary "The dimension of a measurement.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (returns "Base dimension names to nonzero exponents."))

(:doc string->measurement
  (summary "Evaluates an expression with units.")
  (arg text "The expression, as in \"6000 m + 50 ft\".")
  (key table "The table. The standard table by default.")
  (returns "The measurement, or what is wrong with the text."))

(:doc measurement+
  (summary "The sum of two measurements.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second. Raises ArgumentException when its dimension is not a's.")
  (returns "a + b."))

(:doc measurement-
  (summary "The difference of two measurements.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second. Raises ArgumentException when its dimension is not a's.")
  (returns "a - b."))

(:doc measurement*
  (summary "The product of two measurements.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "a × b, with the dimensions multiplied."))

(:doc measurement/
  (summary "The quotient of two measurements.")
  (arg a "The dividend.")
  (arg b "The divisor.")
  (returns "a / b, with the dimensions divided."))

(:doc measurement-scale
  (summary "A measurement times a number.")
  (arg k "The number.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (returns "k × m, of m's dimension."))

(:doc measurement-expt
  (summary "A measurement to a rational power.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (arg q "The exponent.")
  (returns "m^q, with the dimension's exponents times q."))

(:doc measurement-sqrt
  (summary "The square root of a measurement.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (returns "m^(1/2)."))

(:doc conformable?
  (summary "Whether two measurements have the same dimension.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "#t when a and b can be added."))

(:doc measurement-in
  (summary "A measurement as a number of a unit.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (arg target "A unit expression, as in \"ft\" or \"12 mi/h\".")
  (key table "The table. The standard table by default.")
  (returns "m divided by target, or an error when their dimensions differ.")
  (example """
    (match (string->measurement "1 mi")
      ((Ok m) (measurement-in m "km"))   ; (Ok 1.609344)
      ((Err e) (Err e)))
    """))

(:doc measurement->string
  (summary "A measurement as text, in a unit chosen for its dimension.")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (key table "The table. The standard table by default.")
  (returns "The value with 7 significant digits, a space and a unit.")
  (reference """
    The unit is the dimension's preferred unit with a prefix by magnitude,
    as in @code{3.6 MJ} and @code{5 ms}, or else the coherent unit written
    with the base units, as in @code{27.77778 m/s}. A dimensionless
    measurement is the number alone.
    """))

(:doc measurement->string/mixed
  (summary "A measurement as a sum of units: \"5 h 25 min\".")
  (arg m "The measurement.")
  (arg units "Unit expressions, largest first. Each but the last takes a whole number.")
  (key table "The table. The standard table by default.")
  (returns "The text, or an error when a unit's dimension is not m's."))

(:doc convert-query
  (summary "Evaluates a query as Umits takes it.")
  (arg query "An expression, optionally followed by in and one or more units separated by commas.")
  (key table "The table. The standard table by default.")
  (returns "The result as text, or what is wrong with the query.")
  (reference """
    Commas on the left add: @code{6 ft, 2 in in m}. Several units on the
    right give mixed output: @code{1.8796 m in ft,in}. A single unit of
    another dimension divides, and the quotient is printed as
    @code{measurement->string} prints: @code{65 mi in 12 mi/h} is
    @code{19500 s}. Without in, the result is printed as
    @code{measurement->string} prints.
    """)
  (example """
    (convert-query "12 h in s")                   ; (Ok "43200 s")
    (convert-query "65 mi/(12 mi/h) in h,min")    ; (Ok "5 h 25 min")
    (convert-query "1 m + 1 s")                   ; (Err "cannot add length and time")
    """))

(:doc dims->string
  (summary "A dimension as text.")
  (arg d "Base dimension names to exponents.")
  (returns "\"length/time\", \"mass/(length time^2)\", or \"dimensionless\"."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Temperatures
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Celsius
  (summary "An absolute temperature in degrees Celsius. A newtype over double."))

(:doc Fahrenheit
  (summary "An absolute temperature in degrees Fahrenheit. A newtype over double."))

(:doc celsius->kelvin
  (summary "A Celsius temperature in kelvin.")
  (arg c "The temperature.")
  (returns "A measurement of temperature: 20 °C is 293.15 K."))

(:doc kelvin->celsius
  (summary "A temperature in Celsius.")
  (arg k "A measurement of temperature. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "The Celsius temperature."))

(:doc fahrenheit->kelvin
  (summary "A Fahrenheit temperature in kelvin.")
  (arg f "The temperature.")
  (returns "A measurement of temperature: 32 °F is 273.15 K."))

(:doc kelvin->fahrenheit
  (summary "A temperature in Fahrenheit.")
  (arg k "A measurement of temperature. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "The Fahrenheit temperature."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Logarithmic
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc dBW
  (summary "A power in decibels relative to 1 W.")
  (arg p "A measurement of power. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "10 log10(p / 1 W)."))

(:doc dBm
  (summary "A power in decibels relative to 1 mW.")
  (arg p "A measurement of power. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "10 log10(p / 1 mW)."))

(:doc dBV
  (summary "A voltage in decibels relative to 1 V.")
  (arg v "A measurement of voltage. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "20 log10(v / 1 V)."))

(:doc dBSPL
  (summary "A sound pressure in decibels relative to 20 µPa.")
  (arg p "A measurement of pressure. Raises ArgumentException for any other dimension.")
  (returns "20 log10(p / 20 µPa)."))

(:doc dBW->power
  (summary "The power a dBW level is.")
  (arg x "The level.")
  (returns "1 W × 10^(x/10)."))

(:doc dBm->power
  (summary "The power a dBm level is.")
  (arg x "The level.")
  (returns "1 mW × 10^(x/10)."))

(:doc dBV->voltage
  (summary "The voltage a dBV level is.")
  (arg x "The level.")
  (returns "1 V × 10^(x/20)."))

(:doc dBSPL->pressure
  (summary "The sound pressure a dBSPL level is.")
  (arg x "The level.")
  (returns "20 µPa × 10^(x/20)."))
